
  Starting the questions. First one is upstream of everything else:

  [1mWhere does the userId come from?[0m Right now nothing in this codebase produces one. The form collects username and
  password; [38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub that returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m without calling the API. So before we can
  persist a userId, something has to mint it. This choice is expensive to reverse — it sets the shape of what gets
  stored and what every other form reads, and switching later means migrating whatever's already persisted.

  - [3mServer returns it in the login response[0m — the real API responds with a canonical user id; [38;5;153mlogin[39m reads it off the
    response. Most correct for an authenticated app, but it means the login stub has to become a real async call (or a
    fake that mimics one) before any of this is testable.
  - [3mThe username is the id[0m — no new concept; [38;5;153mlogin[39m already has it at hand. Cheapest by far, and zero new failure modes.
    But it's wrong the moment usernames are editable or non-unique, and unpicking that later touches every stored
    record.
  - [3mGenerated client-side[0m — login mints a UUID per session. Works with the stub as-is and needs no server, but it
    identifies a [3msession[0m, not a [3mperson[0m, so it can't answer "who logged in" across devices.

  My recommendation is the server-returned id, and the reason is your own framing: you want to track [3mwho[0m logged in and
  have other forms use it. Only a server-issued identity is stable enough for that. The cost is honest — we'd have to
  decide what the stub does in the meantime, which I'd handle as an explicit fake that returns a fixed id, so the
  interface is real even while the backend isn't.
